  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 159 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nOverall, the upper pattern through the next week will promote\ndry/mostly dry conditions, with a trend toward warmer than\naverage temperatures starting Saturday, lasting through next\nweek.\n\nMain forecast uncertainty revolves around whether or not fog\nwill form tonight, lasting into Saturday morning.\n\nWhile low stratus has shown a clearing trend, the vast majority\nof guidance points toward increasing low level moisture content,\nenough to redevelop low stratus clouds into southern Red River\nValley into Minnesota. Within this low stratus deck, fog is\nexpected to develop as a result of low cloud deck \"scraping\"\nagainst the ground. \n\nHowever, the location and density of fog hold low confidence. \nThis lowered confidence mainly stems from uncertainty in whether\nor not low clouds dip low enough to become fog (potentially \ndense) or if low clouds remain just aloft limiting fog. \nRelatively highest chance for fog to occur reside in the hillier\nterrain of lakes country in MN. \n\nOther, more localized, locations within northeast ND could \nexperience fog, as driven by radiational fog processes (compared\nto fog elsewhere driven by advection fog processes).\n\nWhile not hazardous, Saturday will see winds increase out of \nthe west, with gusts to 30 mph forecast.",
